Now I understand calc-energy precisely:

ED = ∫V·I_1C dt / Σ(layer mass), where:
- I_1C = Nominal cell capacity × 1.0
- Area = Electrode height × width
- Mass layers: positive electrode (thick × (1-porosity) × density), negative electrode, both current collectors (no porosity), separator (thick × (1-porosity) × density).
- Electrolyte excluded.

Key levers for ED:
1. Energy: ∫V·I dt — driven by voltage curve and capacity (which depends on I_1C = nominal capacity!). Note: I_1C is the *nominal* capacity current. If the simulated cell capacity exceeds nominal, more energy.
2. Mass: reduce thicknesses of current collectors/separator, increase porosity (reduces (1-porosity) factor), reduce densities... but densities are material constants.

